{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And Leah said: A troop comes (בגד).</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted that he was born circumcised. And according to this reading it may be explained that the fact that the word is spelled without an alef is to allude that he is the tenth among those circumcised, and they are: Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, Reuben, Simeon, Levi, and Judah, Dan, Naphtali, and Gad. And the numerical value of the word בג״ד is nine, to allude that even though those circumcised before him were nine and he was the tenth, nevertheless they are only nine together with him, because he was born circumcised. And the reason a keri is found reading בא גד with an alef, is that HaShem, may He be exalted, placed it in Leah's mouth to call him גד from the meaning of גד גדי, that is, fortunate, of good mazel; and therefore she said בא גד — that is, the successful one, the possessor of good mazel, has come. For the children of Gad were mighty men, successful in wars, as it is written (Genesis 49:19): ״גד גדוד יגודנו והוא יגד עקב״ — that is, he will succeed and prevail over them in the end." "<b>And concerning</b> the inner intention: Leah called him Gad from the root meaning drawing down, for when she saw that she had ceased bearing, she needed to return and draw down again from the place where she had left off when she gave birth to Judah. And through the power of the prayer she uttered — גד גדי, that is, 'let my mazel be strengthened' — she drew down the force of birth and gave birth to Issachar, who corresponds to Binah, as it is said (I Chronicles 12:32): ״ומבני יששכר יודעי בינה לעתים״. And the second son she called Asher, for she prayed with him as well and said, 'may my strength be confirmed, that I may be strengthened and give birth yet again,' and her drawing down would be a paved path (דרך סלולה), from the language of (Proverbs 23:19): ״ואשר בדרך לבך״. And then He gave her Zebulun, who corresponds to the middle line, aligned with teshuvah, a straight band. And from this you learn that Gad and Asher were not named for qualities but named for the prayer. And through the power of the prayer she prayed with Gad, He gave her Issachar, and through the power of the prayer she prayed with Asher, He gave her Zebulun. And this is clear."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {1 "The lemma 'ועל' reads awkwardly as a lemma head; the Hebrew likely marks a continuation ('And concerning…'). Rendered literally per instruction."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:16:33.163906442Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}